t shifts could narrow the margin considerably.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Thom Tillis (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "North Carolina's open Senate seat, left vacant by Thom Tillis's retirement, has become the cycle's clearest Democratic pickup opportunity. Former Governor Roy Cooper leads Republican Michael Whatley in every recent poll, including surveys from Republican-leaning firms showing margins of five or more points, and the broader polling average sits near eight points. Cooper has also raised far more money, holding a large cash advantage heading into the final weeks.\n\nExpert raters unanimously place the race on the Democratic side of the ledger, and prediction markets price Cooper's chances around 94 percent. Republican super PACs have trimmed ad reservations in the state, a sign of concern within the party despite visits from Trump and Vance. North Carolina's history of close races keeps some uncertainty in the forecast, but the weight of the evidence points to a Cooper win.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9425, "margin": 7.94}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.93, "margin": 8.0}]}